# AiNetCodeNavigator MCP navigation

Use AiNetCodeNavigator as the primary tool for C# declarations and static relationships when available. Query only the context needed to answer the current question; reuse discoveries and deepen selectively.

## Targets and entry points

- Discover exposed tools as needed; client prefixes vary. Their current schemas define arguments, defaults and limits. Never invent tools or parameters.
- `targetPath`: absolute existing `.sln` / `.slnx` for source, managed `.dll` / `.exe` for assemblies. Choose the owning solution; clarify only unresolved ownership. Directories, `.csproj` and `.cs` files are unsupported targets.
- Known physical source path and line: read that bounded file range directly. Known declaration: use its returned stable reference. Otherwise select an entry below.

| Need | Tool / selection |
|---|---|
| Uncertain indexed scope | `browse_target` with `view=scope` (source only); physical files: `rg --files`. |
| Project / namespace entry | `browse_target` with `view=namespaces`; narrow project or namespace. |
| Declaration discovery | `find_symbol`: exactly one of `pattern` or `namePatterns` (1–10), or omit both with `extensionOnly=true`; optional kind/project/namespace/signature filters. |
| File / type outline | `get_file_skeleton.filePaths` / `get_context` with `sections=[members]`. |
| Declaration text | `get_symbol_body.symbolIdentifiers`; bounded window, batch related symbols. |
| Selected symbol context | `get_context`: explicit `sections` from `body`, `members`, `uses`, `tests`. |
| Calls / other usages | `get_call_tree.direction`: `incoming`, `outgoing`, `both` / `find_references` (start `depth: 1`). |
| Inheritance / implementations | `get_type_relations` with `relation=hierarchy|implementations`. |
| Discovered use totals and owners | `find_references` with `includeSummary: true` (start `depth: 1`); counts reference sites before paging, not runtime consequences. |
| Dependencies / type owner | `dependency_graph`: exactly one of `filePath`, `symbolIdentifier` / `resolve_type_origin`: exactly one of `symbolIdentifier`, `typeName`. |
| Compiled API / text / extensions | `inspect_assembly` with filters / `search_assembly` with literal `pattern` (`isRegex: true` for regex) / `find_symbol` with `extensionOnly=true` with receiver, pattern, namespace or signature filters. Extension discovery supports source and assembly targets. |

## Bound requests

- `get_type_relations` on a source target can resolve qualified metadata types or exact `T:/M:/P:/E:` declaration IDs, including overload signatures. Hierarchy accepts types only; implementations accepts interface or abstract/virtual members, including events. Source resolution runs first and only `SYMBOL_NOT_FOUND` permits fallback. For ambiguous metadata, repeat the same raw identifier with one returned proven `metadataOwnerPath`; never guess an owner. This source-only selector rejects `src:` references and assembly targets and cannot load an extra DLL. Keep it and all relation/options unchanged when paging. Source results follow up against `implementationOwnerTargetPath` / `subtypeOwnerTargetPath`; external `metadataRoot` references follow up against its separate proven DLL `ownerTargetPath`. A missing physical owner means no invented assembly handoff; a resolved interface declaration can still report no executable body.

- Discovery `project` accepts an exact loaded source project name (ignore-case) or its returned canonical path. Duplicate names require the path; unknown/empty selectors fail, and assemblies reject project. Namespace substring comparison ignores case; signature substring comparison is case-sensitive. Whitespace optional filters mean no filter. `extensionOnly=true` requires omitted/method kind and can enumerate without patterns; receiver filtering requires extension-only. Receiver aliases/qualified suffixes match declared receiver names, without expression applicability or assignability. For an extension name substring use the existing `*name*` pattern. Keep every effective filter unchanged when following cursors.

- Batch related discovery, bodies or skeletons; start with shallow traversal, filters and small pages. Expand only for unanswered questions or reported limits; skip redundant inventories and graphs.
- Reuse public `handoffId` values unchanged in supported identifier/file inputs. Source references use `src:<project-path>|<declaration-id>`; assembly references use `asm:<simple-name>|<declaration-id>`. Retain the returned owner `targetPath` / `ownerTargetPath`, including referenced DLL owners. Never parse, fabricate or replace references with display names or internal IDs. Obsolete `h:` and `i:` values are rejected.
- For tools that expose source scope, select `all`, `production`, or `tests`; generated declarations default to excluded where an `includeGenerated` filter exists. Before claiming absence, check the applicable tool's scope and filters. `get_file_skeleton` has no scope/generated filter and can return selected generated documents. Assembly `includeReferences` is tool-specific and defaults to false; request supported referenced owners only when needed.
- `includeDiagnostics` defaults to false where supported; enable for detailed navigation/reference diagnosis. Analysis omissions remain visible without it.
- `get_symbol_body.startLine` / `endLine` and `get_context.startLine` are one-based declaration-relative positions. Follow the returned next window; for `get_symbol_body`, reuse that item's reference and next `startLine` / `maxBodyLines`, omitting `endLine`. Read outer pages before advancing a window. Body windows resolve current declaration text; do not join windows from different reported snapshots into one body.
- `get_context.sections` must be non-empty and duplicate-free. `members` requires a type and preserves full class-structure metadata; member name/kind filters, `memberSortBy=lines|kind|name`, and source-only `memberScope=all|production|tests` require members. Member scope affects only members; `uses` includes direct symbol references, including method groups and properties; `tests` is source-only and independent of usage scope. Graph counts distinguish nodes, edges and sites; `memberAccess` is non-call evidence, and `staticVirtualOrInterfaceTarget` is a bound invocation with no runtime implementation claim. Supply `usageScope` only for source uses, `includeGenerated` only for source, `includeReferences` only for assembly uses, body-window arguments only with `body`, and `maxResults` only for list sections. Explicit ineffective arguments fail even when set to defaults.
- Source references identify a loaded project's canonical relative path and exact Roslyn declaration ID. The path is relative to the nearest `.git` working-directory root, or the solution directory when there is no marker. It does not select a TFM. If one physical project is loaded in multiple Roslyn contexts, the reference is ambiguous; use a solution/load configuration that selects one context. The error reports owner path, project name, target framework, configuration, and platform for each loaded context. References survive body edits, unrelated/options edits, and runtime restart while that exact declaration remains; rename, signature change, project move, or absence requires fresh discovery. The source `snapshotId` is a separate versioned fingerprint of binding inputs; current disk changes are checked before identity reuse, and `browse_target` with `view=scope` adds configured-framework inventory to its distinct snapshot identity.
- Source-generator identity includes captured analyzer images, their resolvable managed dependency closure, captured culture satellite assemblies with content hashes and normal optional parent-culture fallback, plus the generated documents materialized by Roslyn for the immutable solution. Do not require a package-specific creator allowlist or claim that arbitrary external file and environment reads are tracked. An uncapturable dependency, captured load failure, or unavailable generated output returns `WORKSPACE_DIAGNOSTIC` before identity reuse. Refresh loaded source, additional, analyzer-config, options, references, analyzer images, and satellite images at fresh boundaries; do not reuse generated-output evidence across a changed immutable solution.
- Assembly references identify a metadata simple name and declaration ID within the supplied owner target. Keep the exact returned target for cross-owner follow-ups. A deliberately selected different DLL with the same simple name selects that DLL. Existing generation leases and provenance checks still protect retained analysis evidence.
- Parse reference input from the original argument before broad discovery-name normalization. Trim outer ASCII spaces, strip at most one matching pair of backticks, double quotes, or single quotes, then trim outer ASCII spaces again. Malformed or noncanonical `src:`, `asm:`, `h:`, and `i:` inputs return `INVALID_SYMBOL_REFERENCE`; they never fall through to names or locations. Do not normalize reference paths, IDs, escapes, or prefixes. Existing raw names, documentation IDs, and location selectors remain discovery inputs.
- If a declaration has no exact representable reference, stable-reference availability flags are false, follow-up-tool lists are empty, and nullable reference fields keep their current null/omission behavior. Owner and raw-location metadata remain available where supported. Body availability is independent and can remain available when its text exists. `get_file_skeleton.filePaths` references resolve all declaring regular/generated documents; that tool has no scope/generated filter. In tools with those filters, resolve exact ownership before applying them. Physical path selectors keep their previous base.
- Before changing shared behavior or a public contract, inspect relevant consumers and runtime wiring. Use direct file reads for current editing context; rediscover affected references after changes.

## Control responses and continuation

Preserve the same tool, target and query for polling/paging, including options and page size. Treat tokens as opaque. Response budgets may change independently of query identity.

| Response / next step | Request |
|---|---|
| `operation=running` | Wait at least the returned `retryAfterMilliseconds` (1000 ms), then poll the same tool, target and query with the returned `operationToken`; preserve active `resultCursor` if present; omit `continuationToken`. Running is not a result. |
| `operation=retry`, `completeness=not_applicable` | Wait briefly; repeat as instructed. Persistent failure: diagnose or fall back; avoid busy loops. |
| Outer response page | Use returned `continuationToken`; omit `operationToken` and `resultCursor`. Reconstruct all outer pages first. |
| Next known-result page | Use returned `resultCursor`; omit `continuationToken` and the previous `operationToken`. Poll if running. |
| `RESPONSE_BUDGET_TOO_SMALL` | Retry unchanged request, retaining its active token, with exact offered `minimumResponseBytes` and `minimumResponseTokens` as `maxResponseBytes` / `maxResponseTokens`. If unsupported, narrow a fresh query. |

- `find_references.includeSummary` defaults to false. Its known-site totals and sorted owner-qualified project/file identities repeat unchanged across logical pages; never sum summaries across pages. Partial analysis carries the same omissions.
- Outer pages deliver one immutable response; result cursors page known results without expanding analysis. For `get_context`, each cursor continues exactly one section; preserve the original full section selection, options, explicit argument presence and page size. Another section needs its own cursor or a fresh query.
- Separate outer `completeness`, `resultContinuation` and `analysisCompleteness` / `omissions`; inspect section status too. In the text preamble, absent `resultContinuation` means no domain continuation, and absent `omissions` means no analysis omissions. A fully delivered page can represent partial analysis. Without a cursor, follow `nextAction` to narrow or change a supported traversal/scanner limit in a fresh query. Larger response budgets do not expand analysis.

## Recovery and evidence

- Ambiguity: select by signature, project, file and owner; follow the selected reference. Never choose the first name match.
- `INVALID_SYMBOL_REFERENCE`: repeat discovery on the current target and use a returned reference. `TARGET_MISMATCH`: use the original result's owner and same reference as instructed. `SYMBOL_NOT_FOUND`: rediscover the renamed, moved or changed declaration. `AMBIGUOUS_SYMBOL`: load a uniquely identifying owner/context; do not guess. `UNSUPPORTED_IDENTIFIER`: correct owner metadata or use available raw-location navigation. `WORKSPACE_DIAGNOSTIC`: correct the reported workspace load/configuration issue and retry. A runtime restart or source edit alone does not invalidate an unchanged declaration reference. Expired pages/cursors or replaced-binary evidence: restart that analysis query. Follow reported recovery; never guess a replacement name.
- Mixed body batches and section failures retain useful partial results. Inspect each item/section's status, availability and recovery; never report partial success as complete.
- Prefer semantic navigation for declarations and relationships. Use focused text searches/file reads for literals, comments, configuration, non-C# or unindexed content, unsupported questions, or unavailable/persistently failing navigation. Text matches are leads, not semantic proof; state material fallback limitations.
- Conclusions apply only to the reported snapshot, indexed scope, filters, depth, owner/reference closure and omissions. `browse_target` with `view=scope` is neither a disk inventory nor proof that all configured TFMs were analyzed. Static evidence distinguishes exact, possible and unresolved relationships; inspect evidence kinds before claiming calls or impact. Reflection, DI/runtime dispatch and external consumers require separate evidence.
- `tests` returns static candidates with usage/implementation/heuristic evidence; inspect and run tests separately. Assembly bodies are decompiled text. Stored response pages do not verify later edits.
- Navigation is read-only; use external tools for edits, builds and tests. Host configuration is read at startup; restart the server after configuration changes.
